telltale*.exe
/telltale
*.test

# goreleaser's output. `goreleaser release --snapshot --clean` is how the
# release config is verified without tagging anything (packaging/README.md);
# what it produces is a throwaway build of a version that does not exist.
/dist/

# Turn traces. `/trace <file>` resolves a relative path against the room's
# workspace, so the natural place to write one while working in this repo is
# this repo — and a trace is a measurement of one machine on one evening, not a
# fact about the product. The numbers worth keeping go in STATE.md with the
# conditions they were taken under.
*trace*.txt

# PowerShell's module analysis cache, when it lands HERE instead of under
# %LOCALAPPDATA%, as an untracked internal/council/Microsoft/… tree on Windows.
#
# This block was added believing nothing in this repository spawns PowerShell
# and that the writer was therefore out of reach. That is not what happens.
# MEASURED 2026-08-09, by polling Win32_Process during `go test ./internal/
# council` and resolving the parent chain while every process in it was still
# alive:
#
#	powershell.exe -File …\github-mcp-wrapper.ps1
#	  <- council.test.exe
#	  <- go test -count=1 ./internal/council
#
#	cmd.exe /c codex exec --json -s danger-full-access --skip-git-repo-check -
#	  <- council.test.exe
#	  <- go test -count=1 ./internal/council
#
# So the suite starts more than git and the Go test helper: it starts a real
# vendor CLI. That vendor then runs the operator's own hooks, which spawn
# PowerShell, and those inherit the test binary's working directory — which is
# the package directory — and write their cache into it. The crumbs are residue
# from a real vendor turn, not litter from an unrelated tool.
#
# Redirecting PSModuleAnalysisCachePath would hide that rather than fix it, so
# it is deliberately NOT done here. The fix is to stop the suite reaching a real
# vendor: startProcess, startSession and startRPCSession are behind package vars
# so tests can stub them (countSpawns does), and the leak says some path still
# gets past. Pinning down WHICH test is open work — output buffering made the
# test name unreliable, while the ancestry above is not in doubt.
#
# Until that lands this stays a backstop and not a fix: it keeps the crumbs out
# of `git status` (and out of an /adopt, the way .claude/ above once broke one)
# rather than stopping them being written.
Microsoft/Windows/PowerShell/

# Claude Code's per-repo settings (approvals, local config). Machine-local
# state, never content this repo ships — and the first live /adopt was
# blocked by exactly this directory sitting untracked in the room tree.
.claude/
